win32u: a window has no buttons, because it has no frame to put them on.

0065 and 0067 leave the client covering the whole window, so Wine draws no
caption and no border. The minimise, maximise and close buttons still showing
are drawn by the programs themselves inside their own client area -- VS Code in
its DOM title bar, Pale Moon in its own -- so nothing Wine paints is involved,
and nothing Wine paints can remove them.

What they all consult is the window style. Electron NativeWindowViews
IsMaximizable and IsMinimizable read WS_MAXIMIZEBOX and WS_MINIMIZEBOX off the
HWND and the Gecko widget asks the same way, so the style is the one lever they
share.

Masked on the way out rather than cleared on the window. 0010 clears
WS_CAPTION|WS_THICKFRAME at NtUserCreateWindowEx and Chromium sets them
straight back -- 0x6CC0000 by the time a window is shown -- so a stored bit is
not something this can rely on. A program that asks is told no every time,
whatever it has stored.

Only what a program reads: the four NtUserCallHwndParam_GetWindowLong entries.
Wine own code calls get_window_long directly and still sees the real style, so
hit testing, SetWindowPos and the non-client paths are unchanged. That
separation matters, because these bits also decide how a window answers a
maximise request and not only how it is drawn.

Nothing is lost: the task bar carries minimise, close and force-close, and move
and resize are handles the shell draws -- the same division 0010 records for the
caption.

Top-level windows only. A child window has no frame and no buttons, and
WS_SYSMENU on one means something else entirely.

Uncertain by construction and worth saying so: this removes the buttons only for
programs that ask before drawing. One that draws them unconditionally will keep
them, and would need its own setting instead.

--- a/dlls/win32u/window.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/window.c
@@ -1294,6 +1294,44 @@
     return retval;
 }
 
+/* Vessel: a window has no buttons, because it has no frame to put them on.
+ *
+ * 0065 and 0067 leave the client covering the whole window, so Wine draws no
+ * caption and no border. The minimise, maximise and close buttons that remain
+ * are drawn by the programs themselves, inside their own client area: VS Code
+ * puts them in its DOM title bar, Pale Moon in its own. Nothing Wine paints is
+ * involved, so nothing Wine paints can remove them.
+ *
+ * What both consult is the window style. Electron NativeWindowViews
+ * IsMaximizable and IsMinimizable read WS_MAXIMIZEBOX and WS_MINIMIZEBOX off
+ * the HWND, and the Gecko widget asks the same question the same way, so the
+ * style is the one lever every one of them shares.
+ *
+ * Masked on the way out rather than cleared on the window. patches/wine/0010
+ * clears WS_CAPTION|WS_THICKFRAME at NtUserCreateWindowEx and Chromium sets
+ * them straight back -- style 0x6CC0000 by the time a window is shown -- so a
+ * stored bit is not something this can rely on. A program that asks is told
+ * no, every time, whatever it has stored.
+ *
+ * Only what a program reads. Wine own code calls get_window_long directly and
+ * still sees the real style, so hit testing, SetWindowPos and the non-client
+ * paths are unchanged -- which matters, because these bits also decide how a
+ * window responds to a maximise request rather than only how it is drawn.
+ *
+ * Nothing is lost by it: the task bar carries minimise, close and force-close,
+ * and move and resize are handles the shell draws, which is the same division
+ * 0010 records for the caption.
+ *
+ * Top-level only. A child window has no frame and no buttons, and WS_SYSMENU on
+ * one means something else entirely. */
+#define VESSEL_UNDECORATED_STYLE (WS_MINIMIZEBOX | WS_MAXIMIZEBOX | WS_SYSMENU)
+
+static LONG_PTR vessel_mask_window_style( HWND hwnd, INT offset, LONG_PTR value )
+{
+    if (offset != GWL_STYLE) return value;
+    if (value & WS_CHILD) return value;
+    return value & ~(LONG_PTR)VESSEL_UNDECORATED_STYLE;
+}
 /* see GetWindowLongW */
 DWORD get_window_long( HWND hwnd, INT offset )
 {
@@ -6438,16 +6476,17 @@
         return get_window_info( hwnd, (WINDOWINFO *)param );
 
     case NtUserCallHwndParam_GetWindowLongA:
-        return get_window_long_size( hwnd, param, sizeof(LONG), TRUE );
+        return vessel_mask_window_style( hwnd, param,
+                                         get_window_long_size( hwnd, param, sizeof(LONG), TRUE ));
 
     case NtUserCallHwndParam_GetWindowLongW:
-        return get_window_long( hwnd, param );
+        return vessel_mask_window_style( hwnd, param, get_window_long( hwnd, param ));
 
     case NtUserCallHwndParam_GetWindowLongPtrA:
-        return get_window_long_ptr( hwnd, param, TRUE );
+        return vessel_mask_window_style( hwnd, param, get_window_long_ptr( hwnd, param, TRUE ));
 
     case NtUserCallHwndParam_GetWindowLongPtrW:
-        return get_window_long_ptr( hwnd, param, FALSE );
+        return vessel_mask_window_style( hwnd, param, get_window_long_ptr( hwnd, param, FALSE ));
 
     case NtUserCallHwndParam_GetWindowRect:
     {
